1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,720
A laptop build and a CI build of
the same commit behave differently.

2
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:12,605
CI is the automated system that
builds and tests every change.

3
00:00:12,645 --> 00:00:17,367
The commit ID is identical. And somebody
says "but git status is clean on my

4
00:00:17,407 --> 00:00:17,930
machine".

5
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:22,163
A clean git status doesn't mean
the directory equals the commit.

6
00:00:22,203 --> 00:00:27,263
Ignored files, the ones Git has been told
not to list, are present and invisible: a

7
00:00:27,303 --> 00:00:31,442
local .env file of settings, compiled
caches, a stale build/ folder.

8
00:00:31,482 --> 00:00:36,603
Any tool that reads the directory sees the
working tree, not the commit: a container

9
00:00:36,643 --> 00:00:41,274
build that copies the project directory,
a packaging step, a test runner, an

10
00:00:41,314 --> 00:00:42,380
evaluation script.

11
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,336
To reason about that, you need a precise
idea of what the working tree is, which

12
00:00:49,376 --> 00:00:53,178
category each path in it belongs
to, and what git status compares.

13
00:00:53,218 --> 00:00:57,834
That's this video. And one command in the
demo shows exactly what the laptop has

14
00:00:57,874 --> 00:00:59,231
that the commit doesn't.

15
00:01:01,071 --> 00:01:04,853
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

16
00:01:04,893 --> 00:01:09,803
Part 1 continues with the place where you
spend your day: the working tree, the

17
00:01:09,843 --> 00:01:14,439
folder of files you edit. Most tutorials
treat it as too plain to explain.

18
00:01:14,479 --> 00:01:19,263
The textbook opens Chapter 4 with three
CTO questions that all turn out to be

19
00:01:19,303 --> 00:01:20,704
working-tree questions.

20
00:01:21,044 --> 00:01:25,248
Why did a secret scanner find a key
although .env was in .gitignore?

21
00:01:25,288 --> 00:01:28,867
Why does the same commit fail
in CI with a missing module?

22
00:01:28,907 --> 00:01:33,984
And can Git bring back two days of labelled
evaluation data that a cleanup command

23
00:01:34,024 --> 00:01:36,605
deleted? The next four videos answer them.

24
00:01:36,945 --> 00:01:41,809
Hold on to one idea through all four. A
version of a file can live in three places:

25
00:01:41,849 --> 00:01:46,653
in the working tree, in the index, or in
a commit. The index is Git's list of what

26
00:01:46,693 --> 00:01:51,556
the next commit will contain. The working
tree is the only one of the three with no

27
00:01:51,596 --> 00:01:56,341
history and no second copy. Git can rebuild
tracked files in it from the index or

28
00:01:56,381 --> 00:02:01,008
from a commit. It can't rebuild content
that never left it, such as an edit you

29
00:02:01,048 --> 00:02:01,894
haven't staged.

30
00:02:03,734 --> 00:02:05,312
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:07,065
After this video you can:

32
00:02:07,405 --> 00:02:10,723
Define the working tree, and say
what a bare repository lacks.

33
00:02:11,063 --> 00:02:15,790
Classify a path as tracked, untracked or
ignored from the index and the ignore

34
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:16,157
rules.

35
00:02:16,497 --> 00:02:21,365
Explain git status as two comparisons, and
read the two columns of the short format.

36
00:02:21,705 --> 00:02:24,395
Choose the status format
that a script should parse.

37
00:02:26,235 --> 00:02:30,760
What the working tree is. In one sentence:
the working tree is the directory of

38
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,613
ordinary files that you edit, build and run:
one checked-out version of the project,

39
00:02:35,653 --> 00:02:38,270
plus whatever else is
lying in that directory.

40
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:43,385
Now precisely. The glossary defines the
working tree as "the tree of actual checked

41
00:02:43,425 --> 00:02:48,084
out files", which "normally contains the
contents of the HEAD commit's tree, plus

42
00:02:48,124 --> 00:02:51,564
any local changes that you have
made but not yet committed".

43
00:02:51,604 --> 00:02:56,321
It starts at the directory that contains
the dot git folder, called the top level,

44
00:02:56,361 --> 00:03:00,788
and every path Git prints is relative
to that directory unless a command says

45
00:03:00,828 --> 00:03:01,368
otherwise.

46
00:03:01,708 --> 00:03:06,832
A repository can have no working tree, which
is a bare repository, the form used on

47
00:03:06,872 --> 00:03:11,996
servers. Or one, the normal case. Or several,
which are linked worktrees and belong

48
00:03:12,036 --> 00:03:13,178
to a later chapter.

49
00:03:13,518 --> 00:03:18,784
And inside the dot git folder? Nothing. The
working tree isn't stored there at all.

50
00:03:18,824 --> 00:03:23,578
The only trace of it is in the index
file. For each tracked path, the index

51
00:03:23,618 --> 00:03:28,820
remembers which blob the file last matched,
and the file's size and timestamps, so

52
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:32,591
that Git can find changed files
without reading every file.

53
00:03:32,931 --> 00:03:37,990
Three categories. In one sentence: every
path in the working tree is in exactly one

54
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:43,150
of three categories, and the index decides
which. Tracked, if it has an index entry.

55
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:46,467
Ignored, if it has none and
matches an ignore pattern.

56
00:03:46,507 --> 00:03:47,696
Untracked otherwise.

57
00:03:48,036 --> 00:03:52,990
A tracked path is shown by git status when
it differs from the index or from HEAD.

58
00:03:53,030 --> 00:03:57,375
git ls-files lists it. git add .
updates it. git clean never touches it.

59
00:03:57,715 --> 00:04:00,511
An untracked path is shown
under "Untracked files".

60
00:04:00,551 --> 00:04:02,735
git add . adds it. git clean removes it.

61
00:04:03,075 --> 00:04:06,869
An ignored path is hidden
unless you pass --ignored.

62
00:04:06,909 --> 00:04:10,924
git add . does not add it,
unless you force it with -f.

63
00:04:10,964 --> 00:04:13,874
git clean removes it only with -x or -X.

64
00:04:14,214 --> 00:04:19,099
Two consequences follow from the definitions.
"Ignored" is a kind of untracked, so a

65
00:04:19,139 --> 00:04:23,965
tracked path can never be ignored. And
"tracked" says nothing about commits: a path

66
00:04:24,005 --> 00:04:27,366
becomes tracked the moment git
add writes its index entry.

67
00:04:27,406 --> 00:04:30,649
Almost everyone hears "tracked"
as "committed" at first.

68
00:04:30,689 --> 00:04:32,350
It means: has an index entry.

69
00:04:32,690 --> 00:04:37,975
The anatomy of git status. In one sentence:
git status is two comparisons and one

70
00:04:38,015 --> 00:04:42,248
scan. HEAD against the index. The
index against the working tree.

71
00:04:42,288 --> 00:04:46,587
And a scan of the working tree for
paths that have no index entry.

72
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:51,141
The manual describes the same three
parts. The first is what git commit would

73
00:04:51,181 --> 00:04:54,345
record. The second and third
are what you could still add.

74
00:04:54,685 --> 00:04:58,150
The short format puts the two
comparisons in two columns.

75
00:04:58,190 --> 00:05:02,024
The first column, X, is comparison
one: HEAD against the index.

76
00:05:02,064 --> 00:05:06,698
The second column, Y, is comparison two:
the index against the working tree.

77
00:05:07,038 --> 00:05:12,349
M and a space: a modification is staged,
and the working tree matches the index.

78
00:05:12,389 --> 00:05:16,096
A space and M: modified in
the working tree, not staged.

79
00:05:16,136 --> 00:05:20,712
MM: staged, then modified again. A
and a space: a new file is staged.

80
00:05:20,752 --> 00:05:25,997
D and a space: a deletion is staged. A
space and D: the file was deleted in the

81
00:05:26,037 --> 00:05:31,348
working tree, and the deletion is not
staged. R and a space: a rename is staged.

82
00:05:31,388 --> 00:05:36,566
Two question marks: untracked. Two
exclamation marks: ignored, shown only with

83
00:05:36,606 --> 00:05:37,235
--ignored.

84
00:05:37,575 --> 00:05:43,141
Formats for scripts. There are two stable
formats. --porcelain, version 1, looks

85
00:05:43,181 --> 00:05:48,747
like the short format, but it's guaranteed
not to change between Git versions or

86
00:05:48,787 --> 00:05:54,213
with user configuration, and it always
prints paths relative to the top level.

87
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:59,189
--porcelain=v2 adds the file modes
and object IDs that status compared.

88
00:05:59,229 --> 00:06:05,075
Add -z when file names may contain spaces
or newlines: entries are then separated by

89
00:06:05,115 --> 00:06:06,967
NUL bytes and never quoted.

90
00:06:07,307 --> 00:06:12,808
Three details that matter in practice.
Untracked directories are collapsed: status

91
00:06:12,848 --> 00:06:18,348
prints the directory, not the files in it.
--untracked-files=all lists every file,

92
00:06:18,388 --> 00:06:23,686
and --untracked-files=no skips the scan,
which is the expensive part in a large

93
00:06:23,726 --> 00:06:28,821
working tree. Second, status writes. By
default it refreshes the cached file

94
00:06:28,861 --> 00:06:34,227
information in the index and writes the
index back, taking a lock while it does.

95
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:39,279
The manual's advice for background tools
is git --no-optional-locks status.

96
00:06:39,319 --> 00:06:43,714
Third, status is local: a line such
as "Your branch is up to date with

97
00:06:43,754 --> 00:06:48,339
'origin/main'" compares with the last
fetched state, not with the server.

98
00:06:48,679 --> 00:06:52,983
All commands in this video are 🟢 SAFE
except two that appear once: git restore .,

99
00:06:53,023 --> 00:06:56,361
which I'll label when it appears,
and git add, which only adds.

100
00:06:58,201 --> 00:07:03,264
Two pictures now. The textbook's analogy
for the working tree: a workbench in front

101
00:07:03,304 --> 00:07:08,120
of a parts cabinet. The cabinet, the
object database, holds labelled parts that

102
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:11,625
never change. The bench is
where you assemble and modify.

103
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:16,666
Git can lay any stored version out on the
bench again, but shavings and half-built

104
00:07:16,706 --> 00:07:20,539
parts that never went into the
cabinet exist only on the bench.

105
00:07:20,879 --> 00:07:23,981
It breaks in two places. Git
doesn't watch the bench.

106
00:07:24,021 --> 00:07:28,724
It looks only when a command asks. And
the bench may hold things the cabinet has

107
00:07:28,764 --> 00:07:32,814
never seen, untracked files, which
Git will list but doesn't protect.

108
00:07:33,154 --> 00:07:36,205
For status: a warehouse
audit with three clipboards.

109
00:07:36,245 --> 00:07:40,961
What the last signed inventory says: HEAD.
What the next inventory will say: the

110
00:07:41,001 --> 00:07:44,052
index. And what is on the
shelves: the working tree.

111
00:07:44,092 --> 00:07:47,916
Status reads out the differences
between neighbouring clipboards.

112
00:07:47,956 --> 00:07:51,899
It breaks because the middle clipboard
isn't a list of differences.

113
00:07:51,939 --> 00:07:56,713
It's a complete inventory, and status
computes the differences each time you ask.

114
00:07:58,553 --> 00:08:03,627
Three boxes. Content moves right with add
and commit, and back left with restore.

115
00:08:03,667 --> 00:08:08,931
Read the captions under the boxes: the right
box holds every committed snapshot, the

116
00:08:08,971 --> 00:08:13,918
middle box one proposed snapshot, and the
left box has no history and no second

117
00:08:13,958 --> 00:08:14,234
copy.

118
00:08:14,574 --> 00:08:19,571
The first question is always about the
index. Only on the "no" branch is an ignore

119
00:08:19,611 --> 00:08:24,117
pattern consulted. Keep that order in
mind. The next video is built on it.

120
00:08:33,457 --> 00:08:38,595
git rev-parse answers "where am I" questions.
From a subdirectory, --show-prefix

121
00:08:38,635 --> 00:08:42,479
prints the path from the top
level to the current directory.

122
00:08:42,819 --> 00:08:48,015
Next, a repository without a working
tree. Predict what git status says in it.

123
00:08:48,055 --> 00:08:49,828
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

124
00:08:54,668 --> 00:08:59,841
A bare repository has the .git content and
nothing to check out into, so any command

125
00:08:59,881 --> 00:09:01,951
that needs a working tree refuses.

126
00:09:02,291 --> 00:09:05,746
Now the asymmetry that the
rest of this block depends on.

127
00:09:05,786 --> 00:09:10,712
The script deletes two tracked directories
and one untracked file, .env, and then

128
00:09:10,752 --> 00:09:12,981
asks Git to rebuild the working tree.

129
00:09:13,321 --> 00:09:18,295
git restore on working-tree paths is 🔴
DANGEROUS in general: it overwrites files

130
00:09:18,335 --> 00:09:22,432
from the index, and content that
was never staged has no recovery.

131
00:09:22,472 --> 00:09:25,628
Video 13 answers the five
questions for it in full.

132
00:09:25,968 --> 00:09:29,958
Here the files are already deleted,
so nothing further can be lost.

133
00:09:29,998 --> 00:09:31,822
Predict: which files come back?

134
00:09:36,662 --> 00:09:40,517
The three tracked files came back
from the index. .env didn't.

135
00:09:40,557 --> 00:09:45,165
Git has never heard of it, and says
so. Tracked content had a second copy.

136
00:09:45,205 --> 00:09:46,924
The untracked file had none.

137
00:09:47,264 --> 00:09:51,062
The repository has four committed
files and a three-line .gitignore.

138
00:09:51,102 --> 00:09:54,111
Four more paths were then
created in the working tree.

139
00:09:54,451 --> 00:09:59,307
Status shows one untracked directory, and
says nothing about .env, data/ or the

140
00:09:59,347 --> 00:10:00,237
bytecode cache.

141
00:10:00,577 --> 00:10:05,181
With --ignored they appear. This is the
command for the hook: it lists what the

142
00:10:05,221 --> 00:10:07,239
laptop has that the commit doesn't.

143
00:10:07,579 --> 00:10:12,379
git ls-files lists each category exactly:
tracked, then untracked and not ignored,

144
00:10:12,419 --> 00:10:13,146
then ignored.

145
00:10:13,486 --> 00:10:18,367
Quick quiz before the next snippet. We
run git add on the ignored file .env.

146
00:10:18,407 --> 00:10:22,900
Option one: it's added quietly. Option
two: it's refused, with a hint.

147
00:10:22,940 --> 00:10:23,936
Say it out loud.

148
00:10:28,776 --> 00:10:33,242
Option two. Moving between categories
is always an index operation.

149
00:10:33,282 --> 00:10:38,689
git add 🟢 SAFE makes an untracked path
tracked. An ignored path is refused unless

150
00:10:38,729 --> 00:10:43,867
you force it. In the short format, A and
a space is a staged new file, and !!

151
00:10:43,907 --> 00:10:49,381
marks ignored paths. The refusal to add
.env is a courtesy of git add, not a lock:

152
00:10:49,421 --> 00:10:51,331
git add -f .env would add it.

153
00:10:51,671 --> 00:10:54,773
One repository, one path
in each interesting state.

154
00:10:55,113 --> 00:11:00,784
src/retriever.py appears twice. That's not
a contradiction: it was staged and then

155
00:11:00,824 --> 00:11:04,405
edited again, so both
comparisons find a difference.

156
00:11:04,745 --> 00:11:06,568
The same state in two columns.

157
00:11:06,908 --> 00:11:11,809
Try it now. For each of these files, say
the letter in column X and the letter in

158
00:11:11,849 --> 00:11:16,689
column Y, from what the long format told
you. Take thirty seconds and write your

159
00:11:16,729 --> 00:11:21,325
answers down. Then I uncover the two
diff commands that perform the same two

160
00:11:21,365 --> 00:11:22,057
comparisons.

161
00:11:26,897 --> 00:11:31,866
git diff --cached --name-status
is comparison one, the X column.

162
00:11:31,906 --> 00:11:36,170
git diff --name-status is
comparison two, the Y column.

163
00:11:36,210 --> 00:11:40,318
src/retriever.py is in both
lists, so its code is MM.

164
00:11:40,358 --> 00:11:43,448
If you had that one, you have the model.

165
00:11:46,788 --> 00:11:52,298
The formats for scripts. Read one version
2 line, the one for src/app.py: the three

166
00:11:52,338 --> 00:11:57,515
modes are HEAD, index and working tree.
The two object IDs are HEAD and index.

167
00:11:57,555 --> 00:12:02,797
They're equal, so nothing is staged for
this path. There's no third ID, because

168
00:12:02,837 --> 00:12:08,348
status compares the working-tree file with
the index entry and doesn't need to name

169
00:12:08,388 --> 00:12:09,150
its content.

170
00:12:09,490 --> 00:12:14,665
And the untracked modes: all lists the two
files inside scratch/, and no skips the

171
00:12:14,705 --> 00:12:14,983
scan.

172
00:12:16,823 --> 00:12:18,471
Five mistakes to watch for.

173
00:12:18,811 --> 00:12:23,144
Taking a clean git status to mean
"the directory equals the commit".

174
00:12:23,184 --> 00:12:28,416
Root cause: ignored files are present and
hidden; git status --ignored lists them.

175
00:12:28,756 --> 00:12:32,967
Thinking "tracked" means "committed".
Root cause: tracked means "has an index

176
00:12:33,007 --> 00:12:35,286
entry"; a path becomes tracked at git add.

177
00:12:35,626 --> 00:12:40,153
Reading a path listed twice as a
contradiction. Root cause: status makes two

178
00:12:40,193 --> 00:12:44,300
comparisons, and a file staged and
then edited again differs in both.

179
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:50,216
Parsing the long or short format in a script.
Root cause: those formats follow user

180
00:12:50,256 --> 00:12:55,628
configuration and may change; --porcelain
is the stable one, with -z for unusual

181
00:12:55,668 --> 00:12:56,373
file names.

182
00:12:56,713 --> 00:13:01,171
Using git diff --exit-code to prove
that a build left the tree clean.

183
00:13:01,211 --> 00:13:06,320
Root cause: it performs comparison two
only, so a generator that created a new,

184
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:08,406
untracked file passes unnoticed.

185
00:13:10,246 --> 00:13:15,115
Now, out of the lab. A CI job has to
prove that the build left the tree clean:

186
00:13:15,155 --> 00:13:19,080
generated code is committed, and
the formatter changed nothing.

187
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:22,856
The job should test git status
--porcelain for empty output.

188
00:13:22,896 --> 00:13:28,079
git diff --exit-code isn't enough, for the
reason you now know: it covers the index

189
00:13:28,119 --> 00:13:32,799
against the working tree and nothing
else, so a generator that wrote a new,

190
00:13:32,839 --> 00:13:34,184
untracked file passes.

191
00:13:34,524 --> 00:13:38,955
And for the laptop-versus-CI difference
from the hook: a build that must contain

192
00:13:38,995 --> 00:13:42,699
exactly the commit should run in a
fresh clone or a fresh worktree.

193
00:13:44,539 --> 00:13:52,347
Your turn. Do Exercise 2.1, Level 1, "every
short status code, made on purpose", in

194
00:13:52,387 --> 00:13:55,467
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

195
00:13:55,807 --> 00:14:00,471
For each file, write the two-letter code
you expect before you run git status

196
00:14:00,511 --> 00:14:04,565
--short, and say which of the two
comparisons produced each letter.

197
00:14:06,405 --> 00:14:10,519
Q5: "Define tracked, untracked and
ignored in terms of the index.

198
00:14:10,559 --> 00:14:13,843
Can a path be tracked and
ignored at the same time?"

199
00:14:16,183 --> 00:14:21,068
Answer out loud. A strong answer defines
all three from one question about the index

200
00:14:21,108 --> 00:14:25,877
and one about ignore patterns, in that
order, and derives the answer to the second

201
00:14:25,917 --> 00:14:30,509
half from the definitions instead of
asserting it. Say which command lists each

202
00:14:30,549 --> 00:14:31,036
category.

203
00:14:32,876 --> 00:14:37,669
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: the working tree is one checked-out

204
00:14:37,709 --> 00:14:42,211
version plus whatever else lies in the
directory, and it has no history and no

205
00:14:42,251 --> 00:14:47,044
second copy. A path is tracked if it has
an index entry, ignored if it has none and

206
00:14:47,084 --> 00:14:49,548
matches a pattern, and
untracked otherwise.

207
00:14:49,888 --> 00:14:54,796
git status is two comparisons and one
scan: HEAD against the index, the index

208
00:14:54,836 --> 00:14:59,294
against the working tree, and a scan
for paths without an index entry.

209
00:14:59,334 --> 00:15:04,692
In the short format the left column is the
index and the right column is the working

210
00:15:04,732 --> 00:15:06,749
tree. Scripts parse --porcelain.

211
00:15:08,589 --> 00:15:12,139
Read sections 4.1 to 4.4 of Chapter 4.

